    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1071">

                <pb facs="0943" n="936" sameAs="frus:frus1937v03/pg936"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.943 Manchuria/31: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-11-18T23:00:00+09:00">November 18, 1937—11
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 18—10:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>551. The British Consul General in Mukden has been instructed to protest to the
                    Manchukuo authorities against the Japanese unilateral surrender of
                    extraterritoriality and to make clear that Great Britain does not recognize such
                    surrender as applying to British subjects. All treaty rights are reserved.
                        Craigie<anchor n="57" xml:id="d1071-f1"/> is to make a similar reservation
                    here. I hope shortly to be able to cable the text or summary of the British
                    note.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1071-f1" n="57" xml:id="d1071-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Robert L. Craigie, British Ambassador in Japan.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
